# Changelog

All notable changes to the BioLink Functional Requirements Specification will be documented in this file.

The format is based on [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/).

---

## [v1.0] — 2026-03-04

Initial release of the BioLink Functional Requirements Specification.

### Added
- Middleware Process Block Diagram (Sampler → Middleware → Analytical Device)
- Sampler functional requirements: sample acquisition, concentration, lysis/homogenization, delivery, preservation, operational control
- Middleware functional requirements: sample reception/routing, reagent management, lab-on-chip control, physical integration, make/break connections, waste management, system coordination
- Analytical Device functional requirements: sample reception, detection/analysis, waste management, data capture, information output, operational control
- Middleware Architecture section defining six functional categories: translation, resource management, contamination prevention, standardization, orchestration, scalability
- Middleware Operating Environmental Requirements (ENV-1.1 through ENV-1.7): temperature, humidity, mechanical environment, pressure, deployment duration, reagent stability
- Eight Critical Interface Functional Requirement sets:
  - IF-1: Sampler to Middleware Interface — volume delivery, sample integrity, contamination prevention, flow/pressure, hermetic sealing, repeatable connection
  - IF-2: Gas and Pneumatic Chip Interface — valve actuation, multi-channel control, pressure stability, gas volume availability, gas cleanliness
  - IF-3: Middleware Reagent Interface — reagent types/storage, volumes/delivery/precision, sequential delivery, contamination prevention, bubble-free delivery
  - IF-4: Middleware and Chip Waste Management — waste containment, waste isolation, capacity planning
  - IF-5: Middleware to Analytical Device Interface — volume delivery, buffer compatibility, contamination-free transfer, delivery method
  - IF-6/IF-7: Electrical (Power and Control) Interface — power distribution, peak power handling, control communication, sensor feedback
  - IF-9: Thermal Management Interface — temperature range/precision, ramp rate, uniformity, thermal cycling, isothermal hold, thermal contact/isolation/power
  - IF-11: Mechanical Docking (Middleware to Chip) — chip retention, hermetic sealing, alignment, connection/disconnection reliability
